{"paper_id": "paper-6b8f9e8a683ffe20", "doi": "10.1007/s44370-026-00055-y", "title": "Narrative review on Marburg virus disease aetiopathogenesis and management", "analysis": {"analysis": {"scope_and_question": "This review focuses on Marburg virus disease (MVD), examining its aetiopathogenesis and management. The target entities include Marburg virus, Marburg disease, and related conditions such as Marburg hemorrhagic fever. The central question is to synthesize current evidence on MVD, covering epidemiology, pathogenesis, diagnostics, and emerging countermeasures.", "evidence_base_and_review_method": "The review is a narrative synthesis of the current evidence on MVD, including epidemiology, pathogenesis, diagnostics, and emerging countermeasures. It draws on recent outbreaks in Rwanda (2024), Tanzania, and Ethiopia (2025), and evaluates investigational therapeutics such as remdesivir and monoclonal antibody MBP091 under expanded access and early-phase studies. No randomised human efficacy data are currently available for MVD, alongside novel platforms including mRNA and adenovirus-vectored vaccines.", "consensus_and_key_conclusions": "The review concludes that Marburg virus disease (MVD) is a highly lethal zoonotic haemorrhagic fever caused by the Marburg virus. It synthesises current evidence on MVD, covering epidemiology, pathogenesis, diagnostics, and emerging countermeasures, such as investigational antivirals and vaccines. Advances in molecular virology have helped to elucidate mechanisms of immune evasion and viral persistence, informing strategies for targeted interventions.", "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": "Despite the severity of the disease outcome, no licensed treatment or vaccine exists for MVD. Diagnostic capacity and therapeutic deployment remain constrained in resource-limited settings. The review highlights the need for strengthening global preparedness through integrated surveillance, rapid clinical trial frameworks, and equitable access to vaccines and antivirals.", "research_and_practice_implications": "Strengthening global preparedness will require integrated surveillance, rapid clinical trial frameworks, and equitable access to vaccines and antivirals. The review underscores the importance of further research into effective treatments and vaccines for MVD, as well as improving diagnostic capabilities in resource-limited settings."}, "summary_en": "This narrative review provides an overview of Marburg virus disease (MVD), focusing on its aetiopathogenesis and management. It highlights the severity of MVD as a highly lethal zoonotic haemorrhagic fever caused by the Marburg virus and discusses the current state of research on diagnostics and emerging countermeasures. Despite recent outbreaks in Rwanda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia, no licensed treatment or vaccine exists for MVD. The review identifies key challenges, including constrained diagnostic capacity and therapeutic deployment in resource-limited settings, and calls for strengthening global preparedness through integrated surveillance, rapid clinical trial frameworks, and equitable access to vaccines and antivirals. It also underscores the need for further research into effective interventions for MVD.", "evidence_ids": {"scope_and_question": ["A1", "A2", "A3"], "evidence_base_and_review_method": ["A5"], "consensus_and_key_conclusions": ["A2", "A3", "A6"], "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": ["A4", "A7"], "research_and_practice_implications": ["A8"]}, "confidence": "low", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "review", "provider": "siliconflow", "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-8B", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_review_anal